= Availability heuristic =

== Overview ==
The '''availability heuristic''' is the use of ease of retrieval, generation, or mental accessibility as a cue when judging frequency, probability, or related properties.

Events that are easy to recall or imagine can feel more common or likely than less accessible events.

== Retrieval as a cue ==
If examples come to mind easily, a person may infer that there are many such examples.

This shortcut can be useful when memory accessibility tracks real frequency.

It becomes misleading when accessibility is driven by vividness, media exposure, recent experience, emotion, or unusual events.

== Availability and recency ==
Recent events can be available, but:

<pre>
availability ≠ recency
</pre>

Old vivid events, repeated stories, familiar categories, or easily imagined outcomes can also be highly available.

== Availability and salience ==
[[Salience]] concerns prominence or processing priority.

Salient events can become more memorable and available, but availability specifically concerns accessibility as a cue in judgment.

== Ease versus content ==
Research distinguishes how many examples are retrieved from how easy retrieval feels.

In some tasks, subjective ease can influence judgment even when the number of recalled examples points in the opposite direction.

== Related concepts ==
[[Cognitive bias]] and [[Salience]] provide the broader bias and accessibility context without equating availability with perceptual prominence.

== References ==
* Tversky & Kahneman, “Availability: A Heuristic for Judging Frequency and Probability” (1973).
* Tversky & Kahneman (1974).
* later ease-of-retrieval research.

